title: Undo and redo by label
tags: [[The operation log]]
kind: reference
audience: dev
status: proposed
summary: Undo offers the labels of the newest operation still standing, and reverts the one chosen; redo does the same over the newest undo; the command line names the label.

!! Undo

Undo looks at the ''newest operation'' that is the user's (not under the watcher's root),
not part of an undo or a redo, and not already undone. The labels it carries — innermost
to outermost — are the candidates:

* one candidate: it is undone at once;
* several: the GUI offers them in the command input's completion, innermost first (the answer, the script run, the work context…), and reverts the one chosen.

Undoing a label reverts every operation under it still standing, newest first
([[Reverting onto the current state]]). On the command line the label is named:
`mf log undo <label-id>`; without one, the candidates are listed. Slower than a single key
for the common case, but it never takes back more or less than meant; shortcuts can be
added where it proves impractical.

The undo is itself a label, "undo of L", linked to L — what `reverts_op_id` says today, at
the label's level.

!! Redo

Redo is the same over the ''newest undo still standing'': its labels are the candidates,
and redoing reverts the undo. Two rules carried over from today (doc "Why undo and redo do
not oscillate"):

* an undo of an undo is a redo — undo never offers an undo, redo never offers a redo;
* an operation is undone only while the revert that undid it still stands, resolved along the whole chain.

Redo steps over undos already redone (today's `plan_redo` stops at the first one — doc
"Why the log stops being a tree"), and offers nothing once the newest user operation is
an ordinary change: a new change clears what could be redone, as in any editor. What it
cleared is still in the log, and an explicit revert of the undo reaches it.

!! Two clients at once — a confusing case

"Newest" is the newest ''operation'', not the newest label. With two clients writing
concurrently — a script running while the user edits by hand —, the newest operation may
be the script's, and undo then offers the script's labels and not what the user just did.
The user documentation must say so; the candidates' names make it visible before anything
is undone.
